# Overlays

Everything that floats above the page is a native `<dialog>` or a
basecoat popover pattern — focus handling, Escape and backdrop come
from the platform. The only question is which overlay fits.

## Choosing an overlay

| Situation | Use |
| --------- | --- |
| A decision that must be made before continuing | `dialog` |
| Confirming a deletion or destructive action | **not** a `dialog` — see below |
| Supplementary content alongside the page (notifications, detail peek) | `sheet` |
| Picking from options attached to a control | `select` / `combobox` popover |
| Searchable command/action list | `command` |
| Outcome of an action | `toast` — see *Feedback* |

If the user does not have to deal with it right now, it is not a
dialog. If it is part of the page's own content, it is not an overlay
at all — put it in the page.

## Dialogs

The `dialog` partial renders a centred, blocking native `<dialog>`
opened with `showModal()`: focus is trapped, Escape and backdrop click
dismiss, the page behind is inert.

- Title states the decision; body carries the minimum needed to make
  it; footer holds the actions (primary + "Cancel").
- **Not for destructive confirmations.** A dialog is dismissible —
  Escape, a backdrop click or the close button close it without
  answering the question — so it must not be the gate on a deletion or
  other destructive action. Use an explicit, non-dismissable
  confirmation for those.
- Long content scrolls inside the dialog body; the dialog itself
  never exceeds the viewport.

## Sheets

The `sheet` partial slides a panel flush to the screen edge (`side:`
right or left) — same native `<dialog>` underneath, positioned. The
header's notification drawer is a sheet.

- Use for content the user consults *while keeping the page in
  context*: notification lists, record peeks, filter panels.
- A sheet is not a form home. If a flow grows fields and a save
  button, it has outgrown a sheet — give it a page.

## Rules

- Never stack overlays: a dialog does not open a dialog; a sheet does
  not open a sheet. An action inside an overlay that needs another
  surface closes the current one first.
- Everything dismissable three ways: Escape, backdrop click, visible
  close control. (The partials wire all three via
  `drawer_controller`.)
- State lives in the page, not the overlay — reopening shows current
  data, and a dismissed overlay must never strand unsaved input.
- One trigger, one overlay: an overlay always opens from an explicit
  user action, never on page load or on a timer.
